# Android overdraw and backgroundColor

On **Android**, every view with a `backgroundColor` (including transparent) can cause **overdraw**: the same pixel is drawn multiple times. Too many opaque or semi-opaque layers hurt performance and can trigger GPU overdraw warnings in developer options.

## When to avoid backgroundColor

- **Fully covered views**: If the view’s entire area is covered by opaque children (e.g. a wrapper `View` that only groups children), do **not** set `backgroundColor`. The GPU will still draw it before drawing the children.
- **Same as parent**: If the background color is the same as the parent’s, set it on the parent only (or the single visible layer) so the pixel is drawn once.
- **Purely structural wrappers**: Wrappers used only for layout (e.g. flex containers with no visible background) should not get a background unless needed for design (e.g. card surface).

## When backgroundColor is acceptable

- **Visible surfaces**: Cards, headers, modals, and any UI that must show a distinct background.
- **Root or screen background**: One background for the screen or root container is fine.
- **Clipping / rounded corners**: If you need a visible “frame” (e.g. rounded corners with a visible fill), one background on that container is acceptable; avoid adding an extra inner wrapper with the same color just for layout.

## Practices

- Prefer **one background per visible layer**; avoid nested views that all set the same or redundant backgrounds.
- Use **StyleSheet** (or design-system props) for background styles; keep the rule “no unnecessary inline style objects” from the styling rule.
- For **clipping only** (e.g. `overflow: 'hidden'`, `borderRadius`), use a single wrapper with background only if the design requires a visible background there; otherwise omit `backgroundColor` on the clipping wrapper.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD – wrapper adds overdraw; same color as parent, fully covered by child
<View style={{ backgroundColor: theme.colors.surface }}>
  <Card /> {/* already has same background */}
</View>

// ✅ GOOD – one background on the visible surface
<Card />

// ❌ BAD – two opaque layers for no visual difference
<View style={[styles.row, { backgroundColor: theme.colors.surface }]}>
  <View style={{ backgroundColor: theme.colors.surface, flex: 1 }}>
    <Text>...</Text>
  </View>
</View>

// ✅ GOOD – single background
<View style={[styles.row, styles.surfaceBg]}>
  <View style={styles.flex1}>
    <Text>...</Text>
  </View>
</View>
```

Consider overdraw when adding or reviewing `backgroundColor` (and equivalent design-system background props) so Android stays within a reasonable overdraw budget.
